 and the display name is typically unset, so the admin UI and policy listings will show an empty string or the group id rather than the group's actual name. Add a parallel branch similar to the users branch that resolves the GroupModel by `resource.getName()` and returns `group.getName()` (and for the \"all-groups\" schema resource return a label such as \"All groups\"). This is a function gap introduced by adding the GROUPS resource type without updating this method."}, {"file": "services/src/main/java/org/keycloak/services/resources/admin/permissions/GroupPermissionsV2.java", "line": 156, "severity": "low", "category": "concurrency", "title": "Missing permission.getResourceId() filter, inconsistent with UserPermissionsV2", "body": "GroupPermissionsV2.hasPermission iterates returned permissions checking only the scope names, without verifying `permission.getResourceId().equals(resource.getId())`. The corresponding UserPermissionsV2.hasPermission does include this check. If `root.evaluatePermission(...)` ever returns a permission whose resource is not the one we are evaluating (e.g. due to policy inheritance or shared scope names across resource types), GroupPermissionsV2 would grant access based on that foreign permission. For parity with UserPermissionsV2 and to avoid potential cross-resource grants, add `if (permission.getResourceId().equals(resource.getId()))` around the inner scope loop."}, {"file": "tests/base/src/test/java/org/keycloak/tests/admin/authz/fgap/GroupResourceTypeEvaluationTest.java", "line": 68, "severity": "low", "category": "style", "title": "Double semicolon on field initializer", "body": "`private final GroupRepresentation topGroup = new GroupRepresentation();;` contains a stray second semicolon. It is legal Java but is a lint-grade mistake worth fixing."}, {"file": "services/src/main/java/org/keycloak/services/resources/admin/AdminPermissions.java", "line": 74, "severity": "low", "category": "api", "title": "registerListener now only registers when FGAP V1 feature is enabled at startup", "body": "The previous behavior registered a ProviderEventListener unconditionally and gated the handler body with a `Profile.isFeatureEnabled(ADMIN_FINE_GRAINED_AUTHZ)` check at every event. The change moves the check outside so the listener is only registered when the V1 feature is enabled. This is functionally equivalent for static feature flags, but it is a behavioral contract change: callers that rely on registering then toggling the feature flag, or that replay events captured before the flag was disabled, will not see the old gating. Either keep registration unconditional (for runtime-toggle safety) or document the new behavior. Not a bug per se but worth a note in commit message / release notes."}]}
